.\"
.\" MOUNT(2) - Substrate Native Personality
.\"
.TH MOUNT 2 "January 2026" "Substrate OS" "System Calls"
.SH NAME
mount, umount \- mount and unmount filesystems
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <sys/mount.h>
.PP
.BI "int mount(const char *" source ", const char *" target ","
.BI "          const char *" filesystemtype ", unsigned long " mountflags ","
.BI "          const void *" data ");
.PP
.BI "int umount(const char *" target ");
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR mount ()
attaches the filesystem specified by
.I source
(often a pathname referring to a device) to the location specified by
.IR target .
.PP
.I filesystemtype
is a string identifying the filesystem type (e.g., "ext2", "minix").
.PP
.BR umount ()
removes the attachment of the (topmost) filesystem mounted on
.IR target .
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, zero is returned.
.PP
On error, \-1 is returned, and
.I errno
is set appropriately.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EBUSY
.I target
is still busy.
.TP
.B ENOENT
A pathname was empty or had a nonexistent component.
.TP
.B ENOTBLK
.I source
is not a block device (when a device was required).
.SH NOTES
.SS Substrate Native ABI
.nf
mount:  Syscall Number: 21
umount: Syscall Number: 22
.fi
.SS Supported Filesystems
Substrate supports: ext2, minix, exfat, udf.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mount (8),
.BR umount (8),
.BR substrate (7)
.SH STANDARDS
Linux-specific.
